            "correct_answer_plain": "therefore watt per amp",
            "correct_answer_images": [
                "V equals I R",
                "P equals V I",
                "V equals P over I"
            ],
            "explanation": "We shall examine each option in turn, recalling that 1 volt is 1 joule per coulomb:\namp per ohm\nWe know ,  which tells us that volts are amp × ohm, and not amp per ohm.\ncoulomb per joule\nThe volt is defined as a joule per coulomb; so here we can deduce that \"coulomb per joule\" is the same as 1 / volt.\njoule per second\nThe joule per second is the unit of power, and the unit of power is the watt.\nnewton per coulomb\nHere it would be useful to express newtons in terms of joules: we do this by noticing that \"work done [joules] is force applied [newtons] × distance moved [metres]\" ; this gives us: joule = newton × metre, or newton is joule per metre.\nAnd so, newton per coulomb is equivalent to joule per metre per coulomb. This is not the same as joule per coulomb and so is not equivalent to volts.\nwatt per amp\nWatt is the unit of power and so we can use ;  this gives which shows us that volt is watt per amp. This is the correct option.\nThe answer is therefore watt per amp.",
