                    "math_alt": [],
                    "plain_text": "loss of gravitational potential energy / J: 100 000, resistive force / N: 1000 / √99"
                }
            },
            "correct_answer": "E",
            "correct_answer_raw": "",
            "correct_answer_plain": "E",
            "correct_answer_images": [],
            "explanation": "The answer is E.\nAs the cyclist loses 100 m of height, he loses gravitational potential energy\n= mgh\n= 100 × 10 × 100\n= 100 000 J\nThe cyclist is descending the slope at a constant speed, which means that all of this potential energy becomes heat as work is done on the cyclist by the resistive forces.\nTherefore, work done by resistive forces = 100 000 J\ndistance travelled along the slope whilst descending 100 m height = 100 × 10 = 1000 m\nTherefore, resistive force = work done / distance = 100 000 / 1000 = 100 N\nAlternatively, since the cyclist is moving at a constant velocity he must be in equilibrium. Therefore the drag force must be equal and opposite to the component of weight acting down the slope (mg sin θ). In this case, sin θ = 10 / 100 = 0.1 so the drag force is 0.1mg = 0.1 × 100 × 10 = 100 N.",
            "explanation_images": [],
            "extracted_at": "2026-07-04T23:57:58.015730",
