            "id": "ESAT-SPECIMEN-PHYSICS-Q11",
            "year": "specimen",
            "paper": "ESAT",
            "module": "physics",
            "question_number": 11,
            "question_text": "An electric motor is used to lift a load of 3.0 kg from rest through a height of 5.0 m in a time of 1.5 s. At the end of the lift the load is at rest again. The motor is connected to a 25 V dc supply.\n\nAssuming that the system is 100% efficient, what is the average current in the motor during the lift?\n\n(gravitational field strength = 10 N kg–1 )",
            "question_images": [],
            "screenshot": "esat_screenshots/physics/q11.png",
            "explanation_screenshot": "",
            "options": {
                "A": "0.40 A",
                "B": "0.60 A",
                "C": "0.80 A",
                "D": "4.0 A",
                "E": "9.0 A"
            },
            "options_detailed": {
                "A": {
                    "text": "0.40 A",
                    "math_alt": [],
                    "plain_text": "0.40 A"
                },
                "B": {
                    "text": "0.60 A",
                    "math_alt": [],
                    "plain_text": "0.60 A"
                },
                "C": {
                    "text": "0.80 A",
                    "math_alt": [],
                    "plain_text": "0.80 A"
                },
                "D": {
                    "text": "4.0 A",
                    "math_alt": [],
                    "plain_text": "4.0 A"
                },
                "E": {
                    "text": "9.0 A",
                    "math_alt": [],
                    "plain_text": "9.0 A"
                }
            },
            "correct_answer": "D",
            "correct_answer_raw": "",
            "correct_answer_plain": "4.0 A",
            "correct_answer_images": [],
            "explanation": "The answer is 4.0 A.\nWork done by the motor in lifting the load through 5.0 m = mgh = 3.0 × 10 × 5.0 = 150 J\nTherefore, output power of motor = work done / time taken = 150 / 1.5 = 100 W\nMotor is 100% efficient so the electrical input power to the motor is also 100 W\nTherefore, average current in motor = power / voltage = 100 / 25 = 4.0 A\nAlternatively, since the process is 100% efficient, the power output of the motor (P = IV ) will equal the power used to lift the mass (P = mgh / t).\nEquating and rearranging we have: IV = mgh / t  so  I = mgh / Vt = (3 × 10 × 5) / (25 × 1.5) = 4.0 A",
            "explanation_images": [],
            "extracted_at": "2026-07-04T23:58:10.351040",
            "options_latex": {
                "A": "0.40 A",
                "B": "0.60 A",
                "C": "0.80 A",
                "D": "4.0 A",
                "E": "9.0 A"
            }
        },
