            "correct_answer_images": [],
            "explanation": "For loss of thermal energy to occur by convection, air in contact with a ball must be heated by the ball. This decreases the density of the air, causing it to rise away from the ball; cooler, more dense air moves in to take its place. For the two balls in the room at 40 °C (R and S), there is no temperature difference between either ball and the surroundings (both at 40 °C), so thermal energy will not flow into the air in contact with the ball, so there will be no convection.\nDull surfaces emit thermal radiation at a greater rate than shiny surfaces. Also, a surface at a higher temperature emits thermal radiation at a greater rate than one at a lower temperature. Ball S has a dull surface and is at a higher temperature than the other similar ball Q, so ball S emits at the greatest rate. (This does not mean that it will necessarily have a greater net loss, because it may also be absorbing radiation. The question is asking purely about emission.)\nTherefore, the correct answer is D.",
