            "explanation": "The answer is: The variation of the wavelength ( y-axis) of waves with a speed of 0.2 m s–1 with their frequency (x-axis).\nThis question is all about the significance of the gradient of a straight–line graph. The gradient of the graph shown is 20 / 100 = 0.20 in the units of the y-axis divided by the units of the x-axis.\nConsidering each option in turn:\nAs F = ma, and therefore a = F / m, a graph of acceleration against force will give a straight line with a gradient of 1 / m. If m = 5 kg then 1 / m = 1 / 5 = 0.2 kg–1. So here, the graphical representation is correct.\nAs V = IR, and therefore I = V / R, a graph of current against voltage will give a straight line with a gradient of 1 / R.  If R = 5 Ω then 1 / R = 1 / 5 = 0.2 Ω–1. So here, the graphical representation is correct.\nAs kinetic energy =  a graph of kinetic energy against speed squared will give a straight line with a gradient of . If m = 0.4 kg then = 0.2 kg. So here, the graphical representation is correct.\nAs v = f λ, and therefore λ = v / f , a graph would need to be plotted of wavelength against 1 / frequency to get a straight line. A graph of wavelength against frequency will not give a straight line. So here, the graphical representation is not correct.\nAs W = Fd, a graph of work done against distance moved will give a straight line with a gradient of F. If F = 0.2 N then the graphical representation is correct.",
