        {
            "id": "ESAT-SPECIMEN-PHYSICS-Q24",
            "year": "specimen",
            "paper": "ESAT",
            "module": "physics",
            "question_number": 24,
            "question_text": "The graph represents the motion of a vehicle during part of a journey.\n\n \n\n \n\n \n\nWhat is the best estimate of the distance travelled during the part of the journey shown?",
            "question_images": [],
            "screenshot": "esat_screenshots/physics/q24.png",
            "explanation_screenshot": "",
            "options": {
                "A": "100.00 m",
                "B": "107.50 m",
                "C": "115.00 m",
                "D": "6.00 km",
                "E": "6.45 km",
                "F": "6.90 km"
            },
            "options_detailed": {
                "A": {
                    "text": "100.00 m",
                    "math_alt": [],
                    "plain_text": "100.00 m"
                },
                "B": {
                    "text": "107.50 m",
                    "math_alt": [],
                    "plain_text": "107.50 m"
                },
                "C": {
                    "text": "115.00 m",
                    "math_alt": [],
                    "plain_text": "115.00 m"
                },
                "D": {
                    "text": "6.00 km",
                    "math_alt": [],
                    "plain_text": "6.00 km"
                },
                "E": {
                    "text": "6.45 km",
                    "math_alt": [],
                    "plain_text": "6.45 km"
                },
                "F": {
                    "text": "6.90 km",
                    "math_alt": [],
                    "plain_text": "6.90 km"
                }
            },
            "correct_answer": "D",
            "correct_answer_raw": "",
            "correct_answer_plain": "6.00 km",
            "correct_answer_images": [],
            "explanation": "The answer is 6.00 km.\nThe distance travelled is the area under the graph, but you also need to match the units. The speeds on the graph are in m s–1 but the times are in minutes. For the area under the graph to represent distance in metres, the times therefore need to be converted to seconds. This can be done all in one go by multiplying by 60 at the end.\nNow consider the areas under each section of the graph.\n0–1 minutes: Area = (16 + 20) / 2 × 1 = 18 (m s–1) min\n1–4 minutes: Area = 20 × 3 = 60 (m s–1) min\n4–5 minutes: Area = (20 + 10) / 2 × 1 = 15 (m s–1) min\n5–6 minutes: Area ≈ (10 + 2) / 2 × 1 = 6 (m s–1) min\n6–7 minutes: Area ≈ (2 + 0) / 2 × 1 = 1 (m s–1) min\n(Note there are various ways of approximating the areas under the last two curved sections of the graph. For instance, another way could be to consider 5–6 minutes as being a trapezium from 10 down to 5 m s–1, effectively importing the last section into the preceding one, and then ignoring the 6–7 minute section. Each method of approximation will produce a slightly different estimate; one such method is shown. All estimates will, however, be within a reasonable range of the key).\nAdding these together, total area ≈ 18 + 60 + 15 + 6 + 1 = 100 (m s–1) min\nAt this point it is now convenient to deal with the necessary unit conversion by multiplying by 60, to give a total area ≈ 100 × 60 = 6000 m = 6.00 km.\n(Note the technique of waiting until the end to multiply by 60 both saves time and avoids dealing with large numbers along the way. It is perfectly legitimate to multiply each time by 60 before working out each individual area, but this will make the numbers more awkward to work with and will result in the question taking a lot longer to do.)",
            "explanation_images": [],
            "extracted_at": "2026-07-05T00:00:53.441907",
            "options_latex": {
                "A": "100.00 m",
                "B": "107.50 m",
                "C": "115.00 m",
                "D": "6.00 km",
                "E": "6.45 km",
                "F": "6.90 km"
            }
        },
        {
            "id": "ESAT-SPECIMEN-PHYSICS-Q25",
            "year": "specimen",
            "paper": "ESAT",
            "module": "physics",
            "question_number": 25,
            "question_text": "A sound wave is produced by a loudspeaker cone, which creates pulses of pressure by moving back and forth between two points X  and Y  as shown in the diagram.\n\n \n\n  \n\n \n\nThe distance between points X and Y is 5.0 mm and the loudspeaker produces pulses of high pressure every 0.20 milliseconds.\n\nThe following statements about the sound wave produced are made:\nWhich of these statements can be correctly deduced from the information given?",
            "question_images": [],
            "screenshot": "esat_screenshots/physics/q25.png",
            "explanation_screenshot": "",
            "options": {
                "A": "P only",
                "B": "S only",
                "C": "P and Q only",
                "D": "P and R only",
                "E": "Q and S only",
                "F": "R and S only",
