            "correct_answer": "D",
            "correct_answer_raw": "m g h equals 1 half m v squared minus 1 half m u squared",
            "correct_answer_plain": "distance z",
            "correct_answer_images": [
                "m g h equals 1 half m v squared minus 1 half m u squared",
                "g h plus 1 half u squared equals 1 half v squared"
            ],
            "explanation": "The answer is distance z.\nLet us assume that the truck has a mass, m and g is the gravitational field strength, v is the final velocity [that is what we want to calculate] of the truck and h is the vertical difference in height between the two positions P and Q. The truck is moving forward at the initial position P with velocity u.\nThe difference in gravitational potential energy between P and Q  = mgh\nThe gravitational potential energy lost on moving from P to Q must be equal to the kinetic energy gained [as the track is frictionless and air resistance is negligible] so if u is the initial velocity, we can write:\nThe value of mass, m, cancels, leaving h as the unknown quantity.\nSo to calculate v we need to know u, g and h. We are told in the question that we know u and g so the additional single piece of information we need is the value of h which is z in the diagram.",
            "explanation_images": [
                "m g h equals 1 half m v squared minus 1 half m u squared",
                "g h plus 1 half u squared equals 1 half v squared"
            ],
