            "explanation": "The answer is 20 000 years.\nThere are originally 1000 carbon-14 atoms in the sample living material.\nThe number of atoms in the sample is 100.\nThe half-life is 6000 years, so the number of atoms after 6000 years is 500. After a further 6000 years it is 250, and after a further 6000 years it is 125 atoms.\nAfter 3 half-lives amounting to 18 000 years, the number of atoms will be 125.\nThis is the nearest estimate to 100 atoms and the nearest estimate to 20 000 years.",
